{"author":{"id":"1949ffc23f11a8cb4f8c42a45c9ad241b49570df9a18687489e9ea78d87c54a3"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1778873961,"nonce":"j4lGm5WyLBlHSCVCP4siEOT7rBM=","title":"Spec: Feedbax Studio pipeline workspace","message":"## Purpose\n\nDefine Feedbax Studio's product-level pipeline workspace: the bottom pane, run/evaluation/analysis/report lineage, stage-scoped specs, collection transforms, and the contract between visible UI state and durable Feedbax specs/manifests.\n\nThis is the Studio companion to `c6c6da0` (manifest/provider/registry contract). The manifest spec defines the durable Feedbax objects; this issue defines how Studio should let a user construct, select, transform, execute, inspect, and report over those objects.\n\n## Current State\n\nStudio already has useful pieces, but they are not yet one coherent product model:\n\n- The top shelf is a model graph editor (`TopShelf` -\u003e `Canvas` + sidebars).\n- The bottom shelf is tab-based (`Training`, `Analysis`, `Statistics`, `Figures`, `Console`) and mixes execution controls, task/loss editing, eval setup, analysis graph authoring, artifact browsing, and logs.\n- `GraphProject` persists `graph`, `ui_state`, `analysis_pages`, and `active_analysis_page_id`; it does not yet persist a first-class pipeline/stage model.\n- `trainingStore` owns a global `trainingSpec` and `taskSpec`, including loss terms and timelines. Those are editable UI state, not stage-scoped project specs.\n- `runStore` models one globally selected training run and eval runs under that training run.\n- Evaluation creation currently takes one `training_run_id`; the backend has partial multi-model hints through `EvaluationRecord.model_hashes`, but the UI contract is still parent-child rather than collection-oriented.\n- `AnalysisPageSpec` persists analysis graph pages, eval params, and an `eval_run_id`; analysis generation logs `eval_run_id` but still discovers data by analysis module key.\n- Autosave covers graph and analysis pages, but not a full workspace draft with training/eval/analysis/report pipeline state.\n\nThe right refactor is not to discard these pieces. It is to introduce a Studio workspace/pipeline model that owns them, then migrate existing panels into stage-specific views over that model.\n\n## Core Decisions\n\n1. The bottom pane is the pipeline/provenance workspace.\n2. The top pane is the structural/scenario authoring surface for the currently selected pipeline stage.\n3. Pipeline stages operate on collections, not one globally active run.\n4. Training tasks, eval tasks, losses, metrics, probes, and analysis inputs are stage-scoped specs with explicit inheritance/forking.\n5. Every visible executable intention in Studio must lower to a typed Feedbax spec before execution.\n6. Executions produce manifests/artifacts; Studio indexes and displays them, but the manifests remain the durable lineage source.\n7. Mandible can enrich/orchestrate this workflow, but Studio must remain fully usable without Mandible.\n\n## Pipeline Model\n\nStudio should add a durable project-level model, conceptually `StudioWorkspaceSpec` or `ProjectPipelineSpec`, persisted alongside or within `GraphProject`.\n\nIt should contain:\n\n- Workspace/project metadata and schema version.\n- A structural/scenario library, including the active model graph and stage-specific scenario variants.\n- Pipeline stages as typed specs.\n- Stage ordering/DAG edges.\n- Collection selections and filters.\n- Draft execution settings.\n- References to emitted run/eval/analysis/report manifests.\n- UI state needed to reopen the workspace, without hiding domain semantics in UI-only fields.\n\nA stage should have a stable ID, kind, label, status, inputs, outputs, scenario reference, execution spec, manifest refs, and UI state. Stage kinds should include at least `train`, `eval`, `analysis`, and `report`; later kinds can include `import`, `compare`, `export`, or project-specific protocols.\n\n## Collection Transform Semantics\n\nThe main workflow pattern is:\n\n```text\ninput collection -\u003e select/filter/group/facet -\u003e apply stage spec -\u003e output collection\n```\n\nThis should be the common mental model for sweeps, evals, analyses, and reports.\n\nExamples:\n\n- Training plan + sweep/replicate settings -\u003e training run collection.\n- Eval protocol + selected training runs -\u003e evaluation result collection.\n- Analysis graph/template + selected eval outputs -\u003e analysis product collection.\n- Report spec + selected analysis products -\u003e report artifact/manifest.\n\nEach stage should default to all compatible upstream outputs, then let the user narrow by run status, tags, parameter facets, metric thresholds, manual checkboxes, or named selection sets. Compatibility should be explicit and validated; the UI should not silently drop inputs.\n\n## Stage Specs\n\n### Training Stage\n\nA training stage should include:\n\n- Scenario reference: model graph, biomechanics/task structure, objective/loss terms, probes, and temporal schema.\n- Training protocol: optimizer, batches/epochs, batch size, schedule/curriculum, seed strategy, checkpoint policy, worker/backend requirements.\n- Sweep/replicate definition: parameters, seeds, Cartesian products or named variants, grouping metadata.\n- Output contract: training run set manifest plus one training run manifest per execution unit.\n\nThe current `TrainingSpec`, `TaskSpec`, `LossTermSpec`, and worker config can seed this model, but should become stage-owned rather than global store-owned.\n\n### Evaluation Stage\n\nAn evaluation stage should include:\n\n- Input selection: one or more model/training-run manifests or a named training collection.\n- Eval scenario: by default inherits the training scenario; user edits fork an explicit eval scenario override.\n- Eval protocol: task variants, perturbations, robustness sweeps, metric/probe requests, deterministic seeds, artifact roles to emit.\n- Output contract: evaluation manifest(s) linked to the model/training inputs and eval scenario.\n\nEvaluation must support different tasks/conditions than training. The UI should make inheritance visible: unchanged training task, inherited-with-overrides, or independent eval task.\n\n### Analysis Stage\n\nAn analysis stage should include:\n\n- Input selection: training/evaluation/artifact collections, with declared required artifact roles.\n- Analysis graph/template spec referencing registered analysis types.\n- Field/selector bindings into state trees, model fields, task fields, metrics, artifacts, and tables.\n- Parameters, transforms, caching policy, deterministic seed behavior, and output products.\n- Output contract: analysis run manifest plus figure/table/report-product artifact refs.\n\nThe current multi-page analysis DAG should be kept as the nucleus, but it should become a stage spec over selected collections. Its current `evalRunId` should be generalized into input collection refs.\n\n### Report Stage\n\nA report stage should include:\n\n- Input selection: analysis outputs, figures, tables, metrics, manifests, and optional narrative/materialized notes.\n- Report transform or template reference.\n- Inclusion/exclusion decisions and ordering.\n- Authorship/provenance for narrative or agent-authored sections.\n- Output contract: report manifest and report artifact refs.\n\nReport-building can be minimal initially. The important constraint is that reports depend on selected analysis/products through manifest refs, not on hidden global UI state.\n\n## Top/Bottom Pane Contract\n\nThe bottom pane selects and configures the active pipeline stage. The top pane renders the structural/scenario view relevant to that stage.\n\n- Training selected: top pane shows editable model + training task + training objective/loss overlays.\n- Evaluation selected: top pane shows model artifact/reference plus eval task/conditions/metrics/probes; inherited training structure is visible but not accidentally mutated.\n- Analysis selected: top pane may show the analysis graph, selected data/source structure, trajectories, field selectors, or artifact views depending on the analysis type.\n- Report selected: top pane may show report composition/preview or selected products.\n\nThis means the top pane is not simply \"the model graph\" and the bottom pane is not simply \"training controls\". They are two coordinated views over the same selected stage and selected collection.\n\n## Time and Scenario Scoping\n\nStudio needs one temporal model shared by tasks, losses, metrics, eval protocols, and analyses. The existing `TimeAggregationSpec` is a useful seed, but it is too narrow for richer authoring.\n\nIntroduce a shared `TemporalSelector` / `TrialPhaseSchedule` concept with:\n\n- Named phases/events/epochs.\n- Absolute index ranges and relative event-aligned ranges.\n- Per-trial/per-batch/per-run aggregation level.\n- Schedules over time or training step.\n- Compatibility with loss terms, metrics, analysis slicing, visualization playback, and report filters.\n\nTasks are not global. A project may have a default task, a training task, multiple eval tasks, and analysis/report selections over artifacts created from them. The spec should represent this explicitly rather than overloading one `taskSpec`.\n\n## Frontend/Backend Parity\n\nThe UI must not create domain concepts that only exist in frontend state. Every executable or durable visual operation must immediately lower to a typed draft spec:\n\n- Drawing/selecting a loss/probe/metric in the structural view updates an objective/metric/probe spec.\n- Editing eval targets or perturbations updates an eval scenario/protocol spec.\n- Filtering run sets updates a named selection spec.\n- Wiring an analysis graph updates an analysis stage spec.\n- Adding a report figure updates a report spec.\n\nThe backend should validate the same specs the frontend displays. Validation should return structured errors/warnings linked to spec paths and UI objects. Executions should use those specs directly or a deterministic lowering from them; no separate hidden interpretation path should diverge from the UI.\n\n## Autosave, Versions, and Drafts\n\nAutosave should persist the whole Studio workspace draft, not just the graph and analysis pages. It should distinguish:\n\n- Draft specs the user is editing.\n- Last validated spec version.\n- Executed spec snapshots used to produce manifests.\n- Current stage selection and UI layout state.\n- Unsaved/invalid/dirty state at the stage and workspace level.\n\nA run/eval/analysis/report manifest should point to the exact spec snapshot used for execution. Later UI edits create new drafts or versions rather than mutating history.\n\n## Standalone and Provider Boundaries\n\nStudio should work without Mandible:\n\n- Build/edit scenarios and pipeline stages.\n- Validate locally.\n- Launch local/remote training, eval, analysis, and report steps through Feedbax.\n- Emit manifests and local artifact refs.\n- Rebuild indexes from manifests.\n\nWhen Mandible is present, it can provide project graph context, review/auth, artifact IDs/storage, agent-generated proposals, and cross-repo provenance. Those are enrichments, not required dependencies.\n\nThe same Studio specs should be usable by an alternative orchestrator through the Feedbax provider contract.\n\n## Implementation Direction\n\nA plausible sequence:\n\n1. Add a versioned workspace/pipeline spec to the persisted project model.\n2. Move global training/task/loss state into a training stage draft, keeping existing UI components as temporary editors.\n3. Replace global run selection with collection/selection state attached to stages.\n4. Generalize eval creation from one `training_run_id` to selected model/run collections.\n5. Make analysis pages reference input collection specs, not only an `eval_run_id`.\n6. Route analysis generation through selected manifests/artifacts rather than module-key discovery alone.\n7. Move reports/figures/statistics into downstream pipeline stages over manifest-backed products.\n8. Keep compatibility adapters for current `TrainingSpec`, `TaskSpec`, `LossTermSpec`, `AnalysisPageSpec`, and database records during migration.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\nA first serious implementation should satisfy:\n\n- A saved Studio project reopens with pipeline stages, stage drafts, collection selections, and structural/scenario state intact.\n- Training, evaluation, analysis, and report stages have typed specs with schema versions.\n- Eval stages can inherit a training scenario and fork explicit overrides.\n- A sweep produces a training collection; eval/analysis/report stages can consume all or selected subsets.\n- Validation errors can be traced to visible UI objects and spec paths.\n- Executions reference the exact spec snapshot used and emit/link manifests.\n- Studio can run without Mandible while still producing portable specs/manifests.\n\n## Open Decisions for Implementation-Time Feedback\n\nThese do not block the spec, but future agents should ask for preference before making irreversible choices:\n\n- Whether the bottom pane should look like a horizontal timeline, node graph, table-plus-detail view, or a hybrid.\n- How much report-building belongs in the first Studio refactor.\n- Whether one evaluation manifest should batch many models or whether Studio should present a collection of per-model evaluation manifests under one eval stage.\n- How aggressive to be in migrating old database records and current analysis pages into the new pipeline model.\n- Which collection filtering/faceting interactions are worth implementing first.\n\n## Related Issues\n\n- `93f79f2` — Feedbax Studio design coordination.\n- `8d94424` — Feedbax-wide design coordination.\n- `c6c6da0` — Feedbax manifests and provider contract.\n- `4e0d925` — original bottom-pane collection-transform issue.\n- `0cee415` — analysis page persistence and multi-page data model.\n- `d90b3e5` — training runs vs sessions/tabs.\n- `e8ec78e` — unified analysis/model canvas long-term direction.\n\n---\nCo-Authored-By: Codex (GPT-5) \u003ccodex@openai.com\u003e","files":null}]}